::U6L2Q01::Alcohol in beverages is {=ethanol ~nicotine ~oxygen ~caffeine}

::U6L2Q02::Alcohol is classified as {=a central-nervous-system depressant ~a stimulant only ~a vitamin ~an antibiotic}

::U6L2Q03::In the United States, a standard drink contains about {=14 grams of pure alcohol ~14 grams of sugar ~one full container of any size ~the same volume for every beverage}

::U6L2Q04::BAC describes {=the concentration of alcohol in blood ~a personal safe limit ~how awake someone feels ~the beverage brand}

::U6L2Q05::Impairment may affect {=judgment, coordination, reaction time, and attention ~only taste ~only legal knowledge ~none of driving ability}

::U6L2Q06::A person can be impaired {=before appearing severely intoxicated ~only after becoming unconscious ~only after vomiting ~only at one exact BAC}

::U6L2Q07::Which quickly removes alcohol from the bloodstream? {=None of these; time and metabolism are required ~Coffee ~A cold shower ~Exercise}

::U6L2Q08::Container size alone {=does not establish the amount of pure alcohol ~always equals one standard drink ~proves safety ~determines BAC}

::U6L2Q09::Which is a short-term alcohol risk? {=Impaired judgment and injury ~Guaranteed recovery ~Improved reaction time ~Eliminated stress}

::U6L2Q10::Repeated alcohol misuse can increase risk for {=liver, cardiovascular, cancer, and neurological harm ~instant immunity ~better coordination ~no dependence}

::U6L2Q11::An intended driver drank alcohol but says “I feel fine.” The safest response is {=do not drive or ride with that driver; arrange a sober safe ride ~accept the claim ~give coffee and wait briefly ~test walking ability}

::U6L2Q12::Why is “I feel fine” insufficient evidence? {=Self-perception does not reliably establish unimpaired driving ability ~Feelings determine BAC exactly ~Coordination cannot be affected ~Alcohol improves judgment}

::U6L2Q13::A peer says “everyone drinks.” This is best evaluated as {=an unsupported perceived-norm claim ~proof of safety ~a medical diagnosis ~a legal exception}

::U6L2Q14::A strong refusal is {=“No, I’m not drinking. I’m leaving with my sober ride.” ~“Maybe if everyone else does.” ~“I will drive afterward.” ~silence while remaining under pressure}

::U6L2Q15::Underage alcohol use may affect {=health, safety, school, relationships, activities, and legal status ~legal status only ~health only ~nothing if no one notices}

::U6L2Q16::Which statement about beer and spirits is accurate? {=Risk depends on actual alcohol amount, not beverage label alone ~Beer is always harmless ~Spirits always equal one drink ~Container size proves dose}

::U6L2Q17::A driver consumed alcohol. Which protective decision is best? {=Use a sober trusted driver or safe transportation ~Ride if the trip is short ~Open a window ~Drink coffee first}

::U6L2Q18::Which can influence BAC? {=Amount, time, physiology, food, metabolism, and other substances ~Beverage color only ~Music volume ~Confidence}

::U6L2Q19::A person is difficult to wake and breathes abnormally after drinking. This is {=a possible alcohol-overdose emergency requiring immediate emergency services ~ordinary sleep ~a reason for coffee ~safe if they vomited}

::U6L2Q20::When alcohol overdose is suspected, the first priority is to {=call emergency services and follow instructions ~contact the TOR first ~let the person sleep ~wait for every symptom}

::U6L2Q21::Why must an unresponsive intoxicated person not be left alone? {=Breathing or choking danger can worsen quickly ~Being alone speeds metabolism ~Vomiting guarantees safety ~Sleep ends overdose}

::U6L2Q22::Vomiting after heavy drinking {=does not make a dangerous exposure safe ~eliminates all alcohol ~proves driving safety ~replaces emergency help}

::U6L2Q23::Which is an alcohol-overdose warning sign? {=Inability to wake or trouble breathing ~Clear judgment ~Normal coordination ~Improved attention}

::U6L2Q24::Combining alcohol with medications or other drugs {=can increase risk unpredictably ~always cancels impairment ~guarantees safety ~is required for treatment}

::U6L2Q25::Alcohol use during pregnancy {=can harm fetal development ~prevents fetal alcohol effects ~is safe based on beverage type ~cannot affect development}

::U6L2Q26::A student wants to protect athletic and academic goals. Abstaining from underage drinking is {=a protective decision supporting those goals ~proof that alcohol has no effects ~an emergency response ~a BAC calculation}

::U6L2Q27::Which analysis separates acute and long-term effects? {=A crash risk is acute; liver disease risk may develop with repeated misuse ~Both are immediate only ~Both are legal issues only ~Neither affects health}

::U6L2Q28::One episode of drinking {=does not by itself diagnose alcohol-use disorder ~proves addiction ~proves dependence ~eliminates need for evidence}

::U6L2Q29::Recurring alcohol misuse affecting responsibilities may warrant {=support from a trusted adult and qualified professional ~public diagnosis by peers ~shame ~driving practice}

::U6L2Q30::Which response is safety-first and non-stigmatizing? {=Recognize the emergency, call for help, and describe evidence without labels ~Blame the person and delay help ~Assume weak character ~Hide the event}
